The Retroactive History of Prophet Wenitte, Mayor of New York City for Life

Historians agree that Prophet Wenitte became Mayor of New York City sometime in the twenty-first century.

What historians cannot agree on is when.

The official records say he was elected after a remarkable political campaign.

The Garas say otherwise.

According to the ancestral archives of Nahouri, Wenitte had actually been Mayor of New York City for centuries before New York City existed.

I. The First Mayoral Term

The story begins in prehistoric time.

Long before Manhattan became an island surrounded by skyscrapers, Wenitte supposedly received his first revelation from the Garas beneath an ancient tree.

The spirits informed him that the future metropolis would require a mayor capable of understanding three things:

1. Human ambition.
2. The chaos of urban life.
3. The mysterious tendency of New Yorkers to complain about everything while continuing to live in New York.

“You will govern this city,” declared the eldest Gara.

“But the city does not exist yet,” Wenitte replied.

“That,” said the spirit, “is merely an administrative technicality.”

Thus began Wenitte’s first mayoral term.

Unfortunately, the term lasted approximately 12,000 years before anyone built Manhattan.

II. The Founding of New York

As civilization developed, Wenitte continued his invisible administration.

When Manhattan was eventually settled, Wenitte allegedly walked its forests and announced:

“One day, there will be apartments here that cost more than entire civilizations.”

The Garas nodded solemnly.

They had seen the future.

Wenitte attempted to warn the inhabitants.

Nobody listened.

They were busy trading.

III. The Arrival of the Skyscrapers

Centuries later, Wenitte returned to Manhattan and discovered that his theoretical city had become enormous.

He stood beneath the growing skyline.

“At last,” he proclaimed.

“At last what?”

“The city has become sufficiently complicated to require me.”

The Garas appeared behind him.

“Your time has come.”

But Wenitte refused to run for office.

“I have no campaign.”

“You do not need one.”

“I have no political party.”

“You do not need one.”

“I have no political machine.”

The Garas laughed.

“You are standing in New York.”

IV. The Election That Happened Retroactively

The campaign began mysteriously.

Wenitte appeared at subway stations discussing metaphysics.

He gave speeches about evolution in laundromats.

He held philosophical conversations with taxi drivers.

He distributed pamphlets containing only one sentence:

“The city is alive.”

Nobody understood the campaign.

Yet somehow everyone remembered hearing about it.

On election night, the results were announced.

Wenitte had won.

There was only one problem.

The election had supposedly occurred three years before he announced his candidacy.

When reporters confronted him, Wenitte simply smiled.

“The Garas warned me that time was going to become complicated.”

V. The Lifetime Mayoralty

Wenitte’s administration immediately became unusual.

He declared that New York should remain:

* ambitious,
* creative,
* chaotic,
* intellectually curious,
* and permanently under construction.

When asked how long he intended to remain mayor, Wenitte answered:

“Until the city no longer requires transformation.”

A reporter asked:

“And when will that be?”

Wenitte looked toward the skyline.

“Never.”

The Garas later clarified the constitutional arrangement.

According to their interpretation, Wenitte’s original prehistoric mayoral term had technically never expired.

Therefore, every subsequent election had merely been a ceremonial confirmation of something that had already been true.

The legal scholars were horrified.

The metaphysicians were fascinated.

New Yorkers mostly asked whether the subway would finally be fixed.

VI. The Immortal Administration

Eventually, Wenitte became known as Mayor for Life.

Not because he conquered the city.

Not because he abolished elections.

But because, according to the mythology, his first term had begun before the city itself existed.

His administration became a strange fusion of municipal government and cosmic philosophy.

At City Hall, plaques appeared bearing mysterious inscriptions:

THE CITY MUST EVOLVE.

NOTHING IS FINAL.

BUILD SOMETHING NEW.

And beneath them was the symbol of the Embodied Felectron.

Wenitte claimed that New York represented the ultimate expression of evolutionary civilization: millions of people, languages, cultures, technologies, dreams, conflicts, inventions, failures and reinventions compressed into one enormous living system.

“The city,” he declared, “is not a machine.”

“It is an organism.”

“And its greatest power is that it is never finished.”

VII. The Final Revelation

On the anniversary of his apparently nonexistent first election, Wenitte climbed onto the steps of City Hall.

The Garas appeared behind him.

Nobody else could see them.

The oldest spirit whispered:

“Prophet Wenitte, your work is complete.”

Wenitte looked across Manhattan.

“Then I may finally retire?”

The Garas laughed.

“No.”

“Why not?”

“Because you forgot the most important law of New York.”

Wenitte waited.

The spirit pointed toward the skyline.

“There is always another term.”

And so Prophet Wenitte remained Mayor of New York City for Life—not because history had stopped changing, but because he had become, in the mythology of his followers, the living embodiment of the city’s oldest principle:

New York must always become something else.

And somewhere beneath City Hall, according to legend, the original mayoral oath remains engraved in stone:

“I, Wenitte, accept this office until the city has finished evolving.”

Beneath it, in smaller letters, someone eventually added:

“So… forever?”

The Garas reportedly answered:

“Exactly.”
